Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 1:08:32 CST
From: david director friedman <ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu>
Subject: PPL: Reputation as enforcement. Predicting the law.

On the subject of bad credit as a deterrent to crime, two points.

1. John Lott, an ex-student of mine now on the faculty at Penn, has
done some work on the punishments imposed by the market. One paper
involved estimates of the lost future income to white collar
criminals as a result of being convicted; as I remember, the cost was
comparable to the legal penalties. Another paper used event studies
to measure the cost to firms of being accused of offenses that
injured their customers. The drops in firm value (measured by stock
price) was many times larger than the legal penalties for the
behavior. I am not sure which of these papers have been published or
where; anyone interested might want to check the Journal of Political
Economy and the Journal of Law and Economics.

2. An anecdote. A very long time ago, Robert Lefevre, a pacifist
libertarian, organized a libertarian get together on Santa Catalina
island. At one point, Lefevre was trying, quite unconvincingly, to
explain how you could enforce property rights without using force
against offenders. Dirk Pearson, who was one of those present, more
or less took the podium away from Lefevre and did a brilliant job of
defending Lefevre's thesis, along the general lines suggested
here--the reputation of being a criminal, hence a bad person to deal
with, costs the offender a lot. I was (and am) still not convinced,
but it was well argued.

A few points on the recent discussion of PPL. One of the things I was
trying to do in Machinery of Freedom was move the discussion from a
question in moral philosophy ("what laws are just") to a question in
economics ("what laws would be produced on a competitive market").
Having done that one can then decide whether one likes
anarcho-capitalism by comparing the law you believe it would produce
with what you believe the law ought to be. My conclusion was that
they would not be identical, but that the market outcome would be
closer to what I think just than the outcome of any plausible
political institutions I could think of, including the present ones.

I am therefor a little disturbed by how much of the discussion here
takes the form of libertarian arguments about what is just. That may
be an interesting and important question, but it is quite distinct
from the question "what would be the outcome of a system of PPL."

So far as the particular question raised about contracts signed by
seven year olds, it will arise in a court case, presumably between
the seven year old and the party who contracted with him. Each of
them will be the customer of an enforcement agency (at least under
the system I describe--I cannot speak for Bruce Benson); the seven
year old either directly or through someone else, presumably his
parents. Those two protection agencies will have a contract
specifying an arbitrator. The rules followed by that arbitrator
either will or will not state that contracts signed by seven year
olds are enforcable. What those rules say is ultimately an economic
decision, determined by what legal rules the arbitration agency finds
that its clients prefer and are willing to pay for.

For a fuller explanation, see part III of the Machinery of Freedom.

On the particular question of damages for violation of copywrite,
someone, I think Mary Read Pickens, suggests that if I pirate your
work and sell to people who would not have bought from you, I do not
owe you any damages. That is not entirely clear. If I had not pirated
your work, I might have licensed it--since it appears that I knew of
a way of making money by copying and selling it. If so, should not
the license fees I would have paid be included in the damages? 

David Friedman

