Date: Mon, 25 Jan 1993 23:50:10 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: DIET: too much protein?

Perry Metzger:
> ...the major effect of high protein intake is to cause your kidneys to
> work overtime dealing with the waste products produced when your body
> (very expensively) breaks down most of those amino acids for energy.

This may be significant, but from what I've read the biggest problem 
is what often goes with the protein: lots of fat, often burned or
rancid, and too much meat in the gut, which is built primarily
like a herbivore's.

The most longevous diet, in my estimation, is low in all three of
fats, carbohydrates, and protein in that order of importance.   
Especially evil are fats, glucose and sucrose.  The stuff that
should be maximized is bulk fiber.  There's no need to go overboard
in being strict about any of these things, except perhaps burned
fats, which in some circles are considered as deadly as the
tar in cigarettes (and that's mighty deadly).  I'm quite interested
in vegan cuisine to substitute fiber for meat, but I see no
need to be strict about it.

I go farther, though, in noting that even a pure vegan diet is
filled with carcinogens.  Bruce Ame's _Science_ article,
reprinted in _The Life Extension Companion_, Pearson & Shaw,
lists dozens of carcinogens in mushrooms, damaged celery, black pepper,
moldy peants & corn, charred toast, etc. in addition to the nasty 
stuff in burned meat.  This stuff is orders of magnitude more carcinogenic, 
than, for example, sacharin.  Much of the damage is believed to happen 
via the action of oxygen radicals.

The free radical theory has been demonstrated in a wide variety of 
experiments, including antioxidant treatment increasing lifespans
and returning the maze-performing ability of old rats to young rat
levels.  So, I consider the normal diet woefully insufficient for 
significant life extension, and take the antioxidants vit. E, C, 
and beta carotene (see previous post for dosage and other supplements 
in my program).  In a few years some of the large-scale, long-term 
antioxidant human trials should start putting out results, but
I'm confident enough to start the program now.

Furthermore, it's important to start soon.  Heart disease is 
being conquered by medicine far faster than cancer.  By 2000, cancer 
will surpass heart disase as the #1 killer; by the time most of us 
are in our 80's, cancer will likely dwarf the risk from heart disease 
and stroke combined.  Cancer has a very long latency period; asbestos 
exposure to workers during WWII caused its peak of cancers in the 1980s.  
So, what we do _now_ to our bodies is critically important to how long 
we'll live.

