Date: Tue, 26 Jan 1993 00:27:11 -0800
From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: PPL:  Born in a PPL

 
>> I still don't see this as forcing the outcome, rather as letting
>> the market operate more naturally; with fewer distortions.
 
>> Mark Read Pickens
 
> Consider an example: the introduction of insurance.  A market
> without insurance is no more forced, no more distorted, and no less
> natural than a market with insurance.  It is, however, different...
> Inventing and marketing insurance thus helps the market "work"
> (produce desirable outcomes), and can hardly be called "getting out
> of the way".
 
> Eli Brandt
 
I hate to keep beating this horse, but I don't think it's stopped breathing.
 This new service (insurance) is being offered to the market, but can anyone
be forced to buy it?  If not, how can a "desirable outcome" be forced into
existance?
 
The option to issue insurance to the market predated the first issuer.  It's
not a "different" market; it's the same market with one additional service
being traded.  What if insurance somehow produced an "undesirable outcome?"
Could we withhold it, preventing that outcome?  If there's a demand, sooner
or later someone else will market it.
 
I suspect that while Eli and I have semantic differences, our  actual policy
positions are virtually identical.  The problem I have with the way he puts
it (making the market work better versus letting it work) is that people
with radically different political agendas can use that same rhetoric to
support anti-free market policies.
 
Mark Read Pickens   autarch@well.sf.ca.us

